 "questions": [
  {
   "xml_index": 1,
   "xml_name": "L05_001_Sum One To N Recursive",
   "display_name": "Sum One To N Recursive",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Welcome to Lab 5! In Lab 4 you saw a first taste of <b>recursion</b> - a function that calls itself. In this lab, we'll practice recursion properly, on a whole series of classic problems.</p>\n<p><b>The most important habit to build with recursion:</b> before writing any code, always ask yourself two questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>What is the simplest possible case, where I can answer directly without recursing at all?</b> This is the <b>base case</b> (or \"termination condition\") - without it, your function would call itself forever and crash with an error.</li>\n<li><b>How can I express the answer to a bigger case in terms of a smaller, similar case?</b> This is the <b>recursive case</b> - it should always move <i>closer</i> to the base case.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Let's practice this on a problem you've solved before with loops: summing all positive integers from 1 to <code>n</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive breakup (the hint formula):</b></p>\n<pre>sum_upto(n) = n + sum_upto(n - 1)</pre>\n<p>This says: the sum from 1 to <code>n</code> is <code>n</code> itself, plus the sum from 1 to <code>n-1</code> (a smaller version of the same problem).</p>\n<p><b>Termination condition (base case):</b> we can't keep subtracting 1 forever - we need a point where we stop recursing and answer directly. Here, that point is <code>n == 1</code>: the sum from 1 to 1 is just <code>1</code>, with no need to recurse further.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>sum_upto</code> that takes one parameter <code>n</code>, a <b>positive integer (int &gt; 0)</b>, and returns the sum of all positive integers from 1 to <code>n</code> (inclusive), using the recursive breakup and base case given above.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>sum_upto(5)</code> should return <code>15</code> (1+2+3+4+5).</p>\n<p><b>Base case example:</b> <code>sum_upto(1)</code> should return <code>1</code> directly (no recursion needed).</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def sum_upto(n):\n    # write your code here (remember: base case first, then the recursive case)\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(sum_upto(5))",
