   "xml_index": 2,
   "xml_name": "L05_002_Factorial Recursive",
   "display_name": "Factorial Recursive",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The <b>factorial</b> of a non-negative integer <code>n</code>, written <code>n!</code>, is the product of every positive integer from 1 to <code>n</code>. For example, <code>4! = 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 24</code>. By definition, <code>0! = 1</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive breakup (the hint formula):</b></p>\n<pre>factorial(n) = n * factorial(n - 1)</pre>\n<p>This says: <code>n!</code> is <code>n</code> multiplied by <code>(n-1)!</code> - a smaller version of the same problem.</p>\n<p><b>Termination condition (base case):</b> we stop when <code>n == 0</code>, since <code>0!</code> is defined directly as <code>1</code> - there's nothing smaller left to multiply.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>factorial</code> that takes one parameter <code>n</code>, a <b>non-negative integer (int &gt;= 0)</b>, and returns <code>n!</code>, using the recursive breakup and base case given above.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>factorial(4)</code> should return <code>24</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Base case example:</b> <code>factorial(0)</code> should return <code>1</code> directly (no recursion needed).</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def factorial(n):\n    # write your code here (remember: base case first, then the recursive case)\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(factorial(4))",
