   "xml_index": 3,
   "xml_name": "L05_003_Power Recursive",
   "display_name": "Power Recursive",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's compute <code>base</code> raised to a non-negative integer power <code>exp</code>, without using Python's <code>**</code> operator.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive breakup (the hint formula):</b></p>\n<pre>power(base, exp) = base * power(base, exp - 1)</pre>\n<p>This says: <code>base</code> raised to <code>exp</code> is <code>base</code> multiplied by <code>base</code> raised to <code>exp - 1</code> - each recursive call reduces the exponent by 1, moving closer to the base case.</p>\n<p><b>Termination condition (base case):</b> we stop when <code>exp == 0</code>, since anything raised to the power 0 is <code>1</code>, by definition.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>power</code> that takes two parameters <code>base</code> and <code>exp</code> (a <b>non-negative integer, int &gt;= 0</b>) and returns <code>base</code> raised to the power <code>exp</code>, using the recursive breakup and base case given above.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>power(2, 5)</code> should return <code>32</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Base case example:</b> <code>power(7, 0)</code> should return <code>1</code> directly (no recursion needed).</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def power(base, exp):\n    # write your code here (remember: base case first, then the recursive case)\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(power(2, 5))",
