   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "L05_004_GCD Recursive",
   "display_name": "GCD Recursive",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The <b>Greatest Common Divisor (GCD)</b> of two positive integers is the largest positive integer that divides <b>both</b> of them exactly, with no remainder. For example, the GCD of 12 and 18 is 6, since 6 is the biggest number that divides both exactly.</p>\n<p>Over 2000 years ago, the mathematician Euclid discovered a very elegant recursive way to compute this, now called the <b>Euclidean algorithm</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive breakup (the hint formula):</b></p>\n<pre>gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a % b)</pre>\n<p>This says: the GCD of <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> is the same as the GCD of <code>b</code> and the remainder of <code>a</code> divided by <code>b</code> - a smaller pair of numbers, since a remainder is always less than the divisor.</p>\n<p><b>Termination condition (base case):</b> we stop when <code>b == 0</code>. At that point, <code>a</code> itself is the answer - there's nothing left to divide by.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>gcd</code> that takes two parameters <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>, both <b>positive integers (int &gt; 0)</b>, and returns their greatest common divisor, using the recursive breakup and base case given above.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>gcd(12, 18)</code> should return <code>6</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Trace through (a=12, b=18):</b> <code>gcd(12, 18) = gcd(18, 12 % 18) = gcd(18, 12)</code>. Then <code>gcd(18, 12) = gcd(12, 18 % 12) = gcd(12, 6)</code>. Then <code>gcd(12, 6) = gcd(6, 12 % 6) = gcd(6, 0)</code>. Since <code>b == 0</code> now, we hit the base case and return <code>a</code>, which is <code>6</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def gcd(a, b):\n    # write your code here (remember: base case first, then the recursive case)\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(gcd(12, 18))",
