  {
   "xml_index": 7,
   "xml_name": "L05_007_Count Digits Recursive",
   "display_name": "Count Digits Recursive",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's write a recursive function that counts how many digits a number has. For example, <code>305</code> has 3 digits.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive breakup (the hint formula):</b></p>\n<pre>count_digits(n) = 1 + count_digits(n // 10)</pre>\n<p>This says: the digit count of <code>n</code> is <code>1</code> (for the digit we're \"using up\") plus the digit count of everything remaining after removing the last digit (<code>n // 10</code>).</p>\n<p><b>Termination condition (base case):</b> we stop when <code>n &lt; 10</code>, i.e. <code>n</code> is already a single digit - it has exactly 1 digit, with no more digits to peel off.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>count_digits</code> that takes one parameter <code>n</code>, a <b>positive integer (int &gt; 0)</b>, and returns how many digits it has, using the recursive breakup and base case given above.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>count_digits(305)</code> should return <code>3</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Base case example:</b> <code>count_digits(7)</code> should return <code>1</code> directly (no recursion needed).</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def count_digits(n):\n    # write your code here (remember: base case first, then the recursive case)\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(count_digits(305))",
