   "xml_index": 9,
   "xml_name": "L05_009_List Sum Recursive",
   "display_name": "List Sum Recursive",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's apply recursion to lists. Since we haven't covered how to build or shrink lists yet, we'll use a trick: instead of recursing on a smaller <b>list</b>, we recurse on a growing <b>index</b> - the same list is passed every time, but the index moves one step closer to the end with each call.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive breakup (the hint formula):</b></p>\n<pre>list_sum(lst, index) = lst[index] + list_sum(lst, index + 1)</pre>\n<p>This says: the sum of the list from position <code>index</code> onward is the element at <code>index</code>, plus the sum of everything from <code>index + 1</code> onward.</p>\n<p><b>Termination condition (base case):</b> we stop when <code>index == len(lst)</code> - we've walked off the end of the list, so there's nothing left to add, and we return <code>0</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>list_sum</code> that takes a list <code>lst</code> and a starting index <code>index</code>, and returns the sum of all elements from position <code>index</code> to the end of the list, using the recursive breakup and base case given above.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>list_sum([4, 8, 15, 16, 23], 0)</code> should return <code>66</code> (the sum of the whole list, starting from index 0).</p>\n<p><b>Base case example:</b> <code>list_sum([5], 1)</code> should return <code>0</code> directly - index 1 is already past the end of a 1-element list.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def list_sum(lst, index):\n    # write your code here (remember: base case first, then the recursive case)\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(list_sum([4, 8, 15, 16, 23], 0))",
