   "questiontext_html": "<p>Now let's find the <b>smallest</b> element of a list recursively - the mirror image of the previous question, using the same index-based technique.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive breakup (the hint formula):</b></p>\n<pre>list_min(lst, index) = the smaller of: lst[index], and list_min(lst, index + 1)</pre>\n<p>This says: the smallest element from position <code>index</code> onward is either the element at <code>index</code> itself, or the smallest of everything <i>after</i> it - whichever is smaller.</p>\n<p><b>Termination condition (base case):</b> we stop when <code>index</code> reaches the <b>last</b> valid position (<code>len(lst) - 1</code>) - at that point, there's only one element left to consider, so it must be the smallest of what remains.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>list_min</code> that takes a list <code>lst</code> and a starting index <code>index</code>, and returns the smallest element from position <code>index</code> to the end of the list, using the recursive breakup and base case given above.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>list_min([17, 3, 28, 9, 41, 6], 0)</code> should return <code>3</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Base case example:</b> <code>list_min([17, 3, 28], 2)</code> should return <code>28</code> directly - index 2 is the last position in this 3-element list.</p>",
