   "xml_index": 12,
   "xml_name": "L05_012_List Contains Recursive",
   "display_name": "List Contains Recursive",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's write a recursive version of a membership search - checking if a value appears anywhere in a list, without using the <code>in</code> operator.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive breakup (the hint formula):</b></p>\n<pre>list_contains(lst, index, target):\n    if lst[index] == target: return True\n    otherwise: list_contains(lst, index + 1, target)</pre>\n<p>This says: <code>target</code> is found from position <code>index</code> onward if it's sitting right at <code>index</code>, <b>or</b> if it's found somewhere from <code>index + 1</code> onward.</p>\n<p><b>Termination condition (base case):</b> we stop when <code>index == len(lst)</code> - we've walked off the end of the list without finding a match, so we return <code>False</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>list_contains</code> that takes a list <code>lst</code>, a starting index <code>index</code>, and a value <code>target</code>, and returns <code>True</code> if <code>target</code> appears anywhere from position <code>index</code> to the end of the list, or <code>False</code> otherwise. Write the full function definition yourself, using the recursive breakup and base case given above.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>list_contains([6, 19, 3, 27, 11, 8], 0, 27)</code> should return <code>True</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Base case example:</b> <code>list_contains([6, 19, 3, 27, 11, 8], 0, 100)</code> should return <code>False</code> (100 is not in the list at all).</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define a recursive function named list_contains that takes:\n#   lst (a list), index (an int - the starting position), target (the value to search for)\n# It should return True if target appears anywhere from position index to the end of lst,\n# or False otherwise. Remember: base case first, then the recursive case.\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(list_contains([6, 19, 3, 27, 11, 8], 0, 27))",
