  {
   "xml_index": 14,
   "xml_name": "L05_014_Is Sorted Recursive",
   "display_name": "Is Sorted Recursive",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Let's check whether a list's elements are arranged in increasing (ascending) order, by comparing each element to the one right after it - using the same index-based recursion technique from this lab.</p>\n<p><b>Recursive breakup (the hint formula):</b></p>\n<pre>is_sorted(lst, index):\n    if lst[index] > lst[index + 1]: False\n    otherwise: is_sorted(lst, index + 1)</pre>\n<p>This says: the list from position <code>index</code> onward is sorted only if <code>lst[index]</code> is not bigger than the very next element, <b>and</b> everything from <code>index + 1</code> onward is also sorted.</p>\n<p><b>Termination condition (base case):</b> we stop when <code>index == len(lst) - 1</code> - we've reached the last element, and with nothing after it left to compare, it can't violate the ordering, so we return <code>True</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>is_sorted</code> that takes a list <code>lst</code> and a starting index <code>index</code>, and returns <code>True</code> if the elements from position <code>index</code> to the end of the list are in ascending order (equal neighbouring values still count as sorted), or <code>False</code> otherwise. Write the full function definition yourself, using the recursive breakup and base case given above.</p>\n<p><b>Example 1:</b> <code>is_sorted([3, 8, 15, 15, 22, 30], 0)</code> should return <code>True</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Example 2:</b> <code>is_sorted([5, 1, 9, 3, 7], 0)</code> should return <code>False</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define a recursive function named is_sorted that takes:\n#   lst (a list), index (an int - the starting position)\n# It should return True if lst[index:] is in ascending order, or False otherwise.\n# Remember: base case first, then the recursive case.\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(is_sorted([3, 8, 15, 15, 22, 30], 0))",
