  {
   "xml_index": 15,
   "xml_name": "L05_015_Recursive Selection Sort",
   "display_name": "Recursive Selection Sort",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>For our final question, let's sort an entire list using recursion - a technique called <b>selection sort</b>. The idea: find the smallest element, put it at the very front, and then (here's the recursive part) sort <b>everything else</b> the exact same way.</p>\n<p>Since we haven't covered slicing lists yet, we need two small list tools to build the \"everything else\" part ourselves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>lst.append(x)</code> adds the value <code>x</code> onto the <b>end</b> of the list <code>lst</code>. For example, if <code>lst = [1, 2]</code>, then <code>lst.append(9)</code> changes <code>lst</code> to <code>[1, 2, 9]</code>. This is how we'll build up a brand new list, one element at a time, inside a loop.</li>\n<li><code>lst.index(x)</code> returns the position (index) where the value <code>x</code> <b>first</b> appears in <code>lst</code>. For example, if <code>lst = [7, 3, 9]</code>, then <code>lst.index(9)</code> returns <code>2</code>. This lets us find <i>where</i> the smallest value is sitting, so we can move it to the front.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>The algorithm, step by step:</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>If the list has 1 or 0 elements, it's already sorted - just return it as-is. <b>(This is our base case / termination condition.)</b></li>\n<li>Otherwise, find the smallest value in the list (you already know how to do this from an earlier question in this lab).</li>\n<li>Find the index of that smallest value using <code>lst.index(...)</code>.</li>\n<li>Swap it with whatever is currently at position 0, so the smallest value ends up at the front. A swap needs <b>two</b> assignments, in the right order: first remember the old value at position 0 (e.g. <code>old_first = lst[0]</code>), then set <code>lst[0] = smallest</code>, and finally put the remembered old value into the position the smallest value came from (e.g. <code>lst[smallest_index] = old_first</code>). If you only do one of these assignments, you will lose a value!</li>\n<li>Build a new list containing everything <b>except</b> the new first element, by looping from index 1 to the end and using <code>.append()</code> to copy each value across.</li>\n<li>Recursively sort that new \"remaining\" list - <b>this is the recursive case</b>, and it is a smaller problem (one element shorter) than what we started with.</li>\n<li>Build and return the final result: a new list starting with the smallest value, followed by every element of the (now sorted) remaining list - again built up with <code>.append()</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> Define a recursive function named <code>selection_sort</code> that takes one parameter <code>lst</code> (a list of numbers) and returns a <b>new list</b> containing the same numbers, sorted in ascending order, using the algorithm described above.</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> <code>selection_sort([5, 3, 8, 1])</code> should return <code>[1, 3, 5, 8]</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Base case example:</b> <code>selection_sort([7])</code> should return <code>[7]</code> directly (already sorted, nothing to do).</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define a recursive function named selection_sort that takes one parameter: lst (a list)\n# It should return a NEW list containing the same numbers, sorted in ascending order.\n# Follow the 7-step algorithm described in the question above.\n# Remember: base case first (a list of length 0 or 1 is already sorted), then the recursive case.\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your function; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(selection_sort([5, 3, 8, 1]))",
   "mark": 1.0,
